<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ezion-Geber</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ezion-Geber was a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a>, at the northern tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>. According to 1 Kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> built ships there that sailed with Phoenician crews to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ophir">Ophir</a> for gold; its exact site is uncertain, with Tell el-Kheleifeh between modern Aqaba and Eilat the most often proposed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezion-geber.jpg" alt="Ezion-Geber" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Creator:Frank Mason Good · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graia,_On_the_Red_Sea,_Near_Ezion-Geber,_Port_of_King_Solomon_MET_DP71226.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ezion-Geber is a city known only from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, which places it in the land of Edom, beside Eloth, on the shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. In modern terms it lay somewhere in the area of present-day Aqaba and Eilat, on the northern extremity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a>, which is also called the Gulf of Eilat. The name appears in some older texts as Asiongaber. According to Targum Jonathan, it means city of the rooster.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Tanakh mentions Ezion-Geber six times. In the Book of Numbers it first appears as an Israelite campsite toward the close of the nation's forty years in the wilderness after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The First Book of Kings presents it as the port from which the ships of Tharshish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and Hiram set out on their voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ophir">Ophir</a>, returning with four hundred and twenty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of gold, and it served as the main harbour of Israel's commerce with the countries bordering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>.</p><p>The Book of II Chronicles tells a later episode. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat">Jehoshaphat</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, joined with Ahaziah, King of Israel, to build ships at Ezion-Geber. According to the account, God disapproved of the alliance, and the ships were broken in the port.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The location of Ezion-Geber is still debated and has not been confirmed by archaeology. The German explorer F. Frank identified it with the ruins of Tell el-Kheleifeh, which the archaeologist Nelson Glueck later excavated. Glueck believed he had confirmed the identification, but a later re-evaluation of the finds dated the ruins to a period between the 8th and 6th centuries BCE, with occupation possibly continuing into the 4th century BCE.</p><p>The question has not been settled. Marta Luciani has argued that old and newly identified samples of Qurayya ware found at Tell el-Kheleifeh show that the site was occupied from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> onwards. Some other scholars identify the biblical port not with Tell el-Kheleifeh at all but with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a>'s Island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ezion-Geber” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hebrew Bible gives Edom seaports at Eilat and <b>Ezion-Geber</b>, and places the &quot;King's Highway&quot; through its land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
